WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.189 --> 00:00:01.614
- Good morning Believe Nation

00:00:01.614 --> 00:00:04.527
today's message is experiment.

00:00:04.527 --> 00:00:06.510
Over to you Bob Ross.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.790
(rooster crowing)

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.074
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:11.074 --> 00:00:14.107
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:14.107 --> 00:00:18.379
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.379 --> 00:00:19.239
- Hardly nothing will teach you more

00:00:19.239 --> 00:00:21.351
than just experimenting.

00:00:21.351 --> 00:00:24.625
Do as many different things
as you have the nerve to do.

00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:26.875
Sure just go for it, there.

00:00:30.494 --> 00:00:32.149
Sometimes you learn more from mistakes

00:00:32.149 --> 00:00:35.022
than you do from trying so hard.

00:00:35.022 --> 00:00:38.743
So any time you make a
mistake, before you get upset

00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:40.410
with it, look at it.

00:00:42.591 --> 00:00:44.502
Sometimes those mistakes those mistakes

00:00:44.502 --> 00:00:49.024
turn out to be the best
learning devices we have.

00:00:49.024 --> 00:00:50.107
There we are.

00:00:51.047 --> 00:00:52.489
I mentioned on one of the other shows,

00:00:52.489 --> 00:00:55.131
maybe even a different
series I don't remember,

00:00:55.131 --> 00:00:56.895
I get letters from people
who say they're just not

00:00:56.895 --> 00:00:58.914
satisfied with what they're doing,

00:00:58.914 --> 00:01:01.202
that their painting is
just, they're just not

00:01:01.202 --> 00:01:04.360
coming out the way they wanted.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.260
I think if there's a
secret to human nature

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.691
it's the fact that we aren't satisfied.

00:01:10.691 --> 00:01:13.253
We're never satisfied,
I hope you're absolutely

00:01:13.253 --> 00:01:17.144
plagued with dissatisfaction
through your whole life.

00:01:17.144 --> 00:01:21.662
Because if you are, you'll
always strive to do better.

00:01:21.662 --> 00:01:23.593
And over and over I tell my students

00:01:23.593 --> 00:01:26.050
if you ever do a painting you're
completely satisfied with,

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.796
you might as well quit.

00:01:27.796 --> 00:01:30.932
'Cause you have nowhere else to go.

00:01:30.932 --> 00:01:32.883
- If you stop experimenting your business

00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:34.549
that's when it's going to fail.

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:37.315
If you just stay in the safe
zone of what has always worked

00:01:37.315 --> 00:01:39.324
and you stopped trying new things out,

00:01:39.324 --> 00:01:41.228
your business is going to fail.

00:01:41.228 --> 00:01:44.243
If you look at anybody
who's had tremendous success

00:01:44.243 --> 00:01:47.505
in their life, in their
career, as an entrepreneur,

00:01:47.505 --> 00:01:49.264
you'll find that the people
who have the most success

00:01:49.264 --> 00:01:51.545
are the ones who experiment the most too.

00:01:51.545 --> 00:01:54.680
They try more stuff, they
fail at a lot more stuff,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.822
and they eventually hit
on things that work.

00:01:56.822 --> 00:01:59.719
This is why I tell you guys
that you need to be prolific.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:02.517
And that whatever you are doing,
whatever you want to learn,

00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:05.634
you need to be prolific at it to practice,

00:02:05.634 --> 00:02:08.293
to experiment, to get better, to fail,

00:02:08.293 --> 00:02:10.358
to learn how to add more value.

00:02:10.358 --> 00:02:12.807
If you're doing something once a week,

00:02:12.807 --> 00:02:15.329
then you're not going to
really get good at it quickly.

00:02:15.329 --> 00:02:17.076
If you want to learn a language,

00:02:17.076 --> 00:02:19.148
you want to pick up a new skill,

00:02:19.148 --> 00:02:21.709
doing that once a week is
not going to be enough.

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:25.668
You'll slowly incrementally
get better at it probably

00:02:25.668 --> 00:02:28.594
but if it's important to you,
if this is a really important

00:02:28.594 --> 00:02:31.188
goal for you then you need to be prolific.

00:02:31.188 --> 00:02:33.081
You want to learn marketing,
that's a thing that

00:02:33.081 --> 00:02:35.380
you really want to do,
marketing's going to be really

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.037
important to your company,
you have a passion for it,

00:02:38.037 --> 00:02:40.302
you need to immerse yourself into it.

00:02:40.302 --> 00:02:42.484
You need to practice,
you need to be prolific,

00:02:42.484 --> 00:02:43.894
you need to try a whole
bunch of different things,

00:02:43.894 --> 00:02:47.483
you need to experiment and
you'll get really good at it.

00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:49.196
But if you're not creating enough,

00:02:49.196 --> 00:02:50.575
if you're not doing enough tests,

00:02:50.575 --> 00:02:52.810
if you're not trying enough,

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:53.909
you're not going to get good at it,

00:02:53.909 --> 00:02:55.802
you're just going to be
mediocre and slowly start

00:02:55.802 --> 00:02:57.683
getting better and better and better.

00:02:57.683 --> 00:02:59.906
And so if it's important for you,

00:02:59.906 --> 00:03:02.831
then you need to practice
and you need to experiment.

00:03:02.831 --> 00:03:04.759
I would also encourage
you not to worry so much

00:03:04.759 --> 00:03:06.808
about getting the perfect thing out.

00:03:06.808 --> 00:03:10.121
Perfectionism stops you from
making any kind of progress.

00:03:10.121 --> 00:03:11.810
It's holding you where you are.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.127
The attempt to try and
make the perfect thing

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:16.583
is an unattainable goal,
it will never be perfect.

00:03:16.583 --> 00:03:17.832
It will always be better.

00:03:17.832 --> 00:03:20.583
There's always a way
to make things better.

00:03:20.583 --> 00:03:22.029
It's never perfect.

00:03:22.029 --> 00:03:24.768
Making this video is not perfect.

00:03:24.768 --> 00:03:28.083
As much as I've done 4,000 videos

00:03:28.083 --> 00:03:29.192
that have led me to this point

00:03:29.192 --> 00:03:30.992
to make this video for you guys,

00:03:30.992 --> 00:03:33.000
I could still get way better.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.145
The Evan from 2020 is going
to look back on this video

00:03:37.145 --> 00:03:40.812
and say man, I was so
bad, what a bad video.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:43.947
Look at what I was doing,
look at how I was acting,

00:03:43.947 --> 00:03:46.276
look at what I was saying,
I'm so much better now.

00:03:46.276 --> 00:03:48.134
I can't believe that video is up.

00:03:48.134 --> 00:03:48.967
And that's fine.

00:03:48.967 --> 00:03:51.623
Because when you are prolific
in your content creation

00:03:51.623 --> 00:03:53.596
or whatever you're trying to do.

00:03:53.596 --> 00:03:56.551
When you're creating so much
and you continue to practice,

00:03:56.551 --> 00:03:59.834
the one video that you
make doesn't matter.

00:03:59.834 --> 00:04:02.246
If I'm trying to practice
learning Italian,

00:04:02.246 --> 00:04:06.205
the one conversation I have
with somebody doesn't matter.

00:04:06.205 --> 00:04:07.430
It's part of a bigger picture,

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.154
it's part of my entire growth.

00:04:09.154 --> 00:04:12.530
The individual piece that you're creating,

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:14.361
you're investing time,
you're investing energy,

00:04:14.361 --> 00:04:15.507
you're investing your heart, you're trying

00:04:15.507 --> 00:04:17.386
to make it the best that you can make it

00:04:17.386 --> 00:04:19.904
with the skills and resources
that you have right now.

00:04:19.904 --> 00:04:20.737
That's it.

00:04:20.737 --> 00:04:21.895
That's the most important thing.

00:04:21.895 --> 00:04:23.991
I realized that tomorrow's
going to be a little bit better

00:04:23.991 --> 00:04:24.960
and the next day a little bit better.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.033
If you continue to hustle,
then in 12, 24, 36 months,

00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:31.129
you're going to be way better.

00:04:31.129 --> 00:04:32.768
And then you're still going to drive,

00:04:32.768 --> 00:04:35.681
you're going to still continue,
there is no end point.

00:04:35.681 --> 00:04:36.912
You want to continue to get better

00:04:36.912 --> 00:04:38.665
because this is an important goal for you.

00:04:38.665 --> 00:04:41.880
And you do it by continuing to experiment.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:43.065
So the question of the day today is,

00:04:43.065 --> 00:04:46.065
I'm curious what new
experiment are you going

00:04:46.065 --> 00:04:49.271
to try today in your business or your life

00:04:49.271 --> 00:04:51.640
that you've been putting off,
you're a little bit scared of,

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.310
you feel you may not be good
enough, or ready for it yet,

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:57.119
how can you start it,
some small experiment,

00:04:57.119 --> 00:04:59.015
leave it down in the
comments below, super curious

00:04:59.015 --> 00:05:02.307
to find out what that is
and by sharing it publicly

00:05:02.307 --> 00:05:04.008
hopefully that gives you the push

00:05:04.008 --> 00:05:05.854
to actually go and do it.

00:05:05.854 --> 00:05:08.374
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Raphael Moore.

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:10.318
Raphael thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:05:10.318 --> 00:05:12.887
of my book, Your One Word,
it really really really means

00:05:12.887 --> 00:05:15.578
a lot to me and I hope you're enjoying it.

00:05:15.578 --> 00:05:17.920
So thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you,

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.881
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:05:19.881 --> 00:05:22.801
and whatever your one word is, much love

00:05:22.801 --> 00:05:24.149
and I'll see you again tomorrow morning

00:05:24.149 --> 00:05:26.511
for another shot of espresso.

00:05:26.511 --> 00:05:28.926
♫ I wake up every morning

00:05:28.926 --> 00:05:32.670
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.104
- Hey Believe Nation
for those of you looking

00:05:34.104 --> 00:05:36.711
for a little more
inspiration to experiment,

00:05:36.711 --> 00:05:39.540
here are some bonus clips
that I think you'll enjoy.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:43.707
- I don't make notes, I don't,
I react very instinctively

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:49.066
to everything, I'm a great believer

00:05:49.066 --> 00:05:50.900
in traveling a great deal.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:54.027
I don't believe meetings,
more than two people

00:05:54.027 --> 00:05:57.610
in a meeting is too
many, really it's true.

00:06:00.205 --> 00:06:02.635
Do you know about stand-ups?

00:06:02.635 --> 00:06:05.539
No okay, so in Silicon Valley
now, when you have a meeting

00:06:05.539 --> 00:06:08.501
everyone stands in a circle
like they're meditating

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:11.834
and that's how, maybe I should try that.

00:06:14.631 --> 00:06:17.275
I think that the more you
go out, the more you see,

00:06:17.275 --> 00:06:19.635
the more you experience
life, the more you bring back

00:06:19.635 --> 00:06:24.035
to your day job so as I was
trying to say in my remarks,

00:06:24.035 --> 00:06:26.020
I think the problem about big corporations

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:30.211
is you get a lot of people
my age trying to hold on

00:06:30.211 --> 00:06:34.745
to their jobs and you know,
clinging onto their pensions

00:06:34.745 --> 00:06:37.588
and their retirement and they're
not experiencing new things

00:06:37.588 --> 00:06:40.637
and they don't like change
and they don't like disruption

00:06:40.637 --> 00:06:42.563
so they're, either they
don't make a decision

00:06:42.563 --> 00:06:45.618
or they say no and as
a result they stagnate.

00:06:45.618 --> 00:06:50.187
So it's terribly important
to bring people in

00:06:50.187 --> 00:06:53.453
from the outside and not
to remain too insular.

00:06:53.453 --> 00:06:56.967
And also to hire as
many young bright people

00:06:56.967 --> 00:06:59.184
like all of you as you
can just to teach you

00:06:59.184 --> 00:07:02.050
how to think about things
completely differently.

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:04.728
- I think the secret to
my career is I don't know

00:07:04.728 --> 00:07:06.410
what I can't do.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:09.915
So, you know, there were
times when I should probably

00:07:09.915 --> 00:07:12.958
sit and think, do I really
want to jump out there

00:07:12.958 --> 00:07:16.540
with Phillip Seymour, I
mean with John C. Reilly

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:21.114
and sing The Night Chicago
Died or do I really want

00:07:21.114 --> 00:07:24.474
to play the blues with
Little Ed and you know,

00:07:24.474 --> 00:07:27.356
you just do it, you just do these things.

00:07:27.356 --> 00:07:29.091
- [Charlie] And you
become, the audience wants

00:07:29.091 --> 00:07:30.667
you to do these things.

00:07:30.667 --> 00:07:33.730
- Yeah I think what's,
I don't know what it is,

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:36.803
but I think over the years
people have gotten comfortable

00:07:36.803 --> 00:07:40.355
with the notion that I try things.

00:07:40.355 --> 00:07:41.395
- It's almost krypton-esque.

00:07:41.395 --> 00:07:43.548
- And they're not worried about me.

00:07:43.548 --> 00:07:44.465
Do you know what I mean?

00:07:44.465 --> 00:07:46.010
I think that's the key.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:49.717
People aren't worried
about me getting hurt

00:07:49.717 --> 00:07:53.717
because I can you know,
I'll give it 100 percent

00:07:55.592 --> 00:07:57.366
and then if it completely
falls on its face,

00:07:57.366 --> 00:07:59.211
I'll laugh about it and move on.

00:07:59.211 --> 00:08:01.179
And I think there's a
little bit of a sense

00:08:01.179 --> 00:08:04.065
after all these years,
people say you know,

00:08:04.065 --> 00:08:07.153
alright he tried it, maybe it
worked, maybe it didn't work,

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:08.843
but he had a good time.

00:08:08.843 --> 00:08:11.153
- And for you, you can
come back the next night

00:08:11.153 --> 00:08:13.635
and the next night and the next night.

00:08:13.635 --> 00:08:17.385
- Yeah, I mean that's
you know, I don't know,

00:08:21.454 --> 00:08:25.822
over the years I've realized
there are a lot of comedians

00:08:25.822 --> 00:08:30.640
that are very worried about
keeping their dignity intact.

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:31.473
- [Charlie] Yeah.

00:08:31.473 --> 00:08:33.560
- And keeping their personal space

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:36.112
and keeping their sense of authority.

00:08:36.112 --> 00:08:37.902
And for better or worse,
and it might be worse,

00:08:37.902 --> 00:08:40.462
I don't have that, you know what I mean?

00:08:40.462 --> 00:08:44.288
I'm not, the people that
I've always liked growing up

00:08:44.288 --> 00:08:45.622
and I'm not just talking
about late night comedians,

00:08:45.622 --> 00:08:47.997
I'm talking about anybody
in movies, in film,

00:08:47.997 --> 00:08:51.813
and the people I liked Peter Sellers--

00:08:51.813 --> 00:08:52.892
- [Charlie] They would
let it all hang out.

00:08:52.892 --> 00:08:55.843
- He'd just go, if you
look at a lot of those old

00:08:55.843 --> 00:08:58.327
classic Carson clips,
he's jumping into hot tubs

00:08:58.327 --> 00:09:00.208
with Don Rickles.

00:09:00.208 --> 00:09:01.041
- [Charlie] Right.

00:09:01.041 --> 00:09:04.823
- He's jumping through fake
walls, he's falling on his ass,

00:09:04.823 --> 00:09:07.156
he's dressed as Floyd Turbo.

00:09:08.306 --> 00:09:11.394
And sometimes I look back
to that and I remember

00:09:11.394 --> 00:09:15.561
well, Johnny just went for
it, so for better or worse,

00:09:17.721 --> 00:09:20.712
I'll try, I'll get out
there and I'm very physical

00:09:20.712 --> 00:09:23.208
and my wife is always
horrified, I'm always coming out

00:09:23.208 --> 00:09:25.983
of the shower just covered in bruises

00:09:25.983 --> 00:09:26.816
and she'll say, what was that?

00:09:26.816 --> 00:09:30.552
And I'll say oh, I did a
bit, we had Jeff Garland

00:09:30.552 --> 00:09:34.451
on the show and he and I
rolled down a flight a steps

00:09:34.451 --> 00:09:36.246
and I hit a fire hydrant.

